 The full-width variant should take up the full width of the container, while the centered variant should be centered horizontally.

Here is an example of how you can achieve this using Tailwind CSS:
```jsx
import React from 'react';

const Layout = ({ children, variant = 'full-width' }) => {
  return (
    <div
      className={`flex flex-col ${variant === 'full-width' ? 'w-full' : 'max-w-md mx-auto'}`}
    >
      {children}
    </div>
  );
};

export default Layout;
```
In this example, we define a `Layout` component that takes two props: `children` and `variant`. The `variant` prop defaults to `'full-width'`.

We use the `className` prop to apply the styles to the component. We use the `flex` and `flex-col` classes to make the component a flexible container that stacks its children vertically.

For the `full-width` variant, we use the `w-full` class to make the component take up the full width of its parent container.

For the `centered` variant, we use the `max-w-md` class to set a maximum width of `md` (medium) and the `mx-auto` class to center the component horizontally.

You can use this component like this:
```jsx
import React from 'react';
import Layout from './Layout';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <Layout variant="full-width">
      <h1>Hello World!</h1>
    </Layout>
  );
};
```
Or:
```jsx
import React from 'react';
import Layout from './Layout';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <Layout variant="centered">
      <h1>Hello World!</h1>
    </Layout>
  );
};
```
Note that you can customize the styles by modifying the `className` prop. For example, you can add more classes to the `flex` container or modify the `max-w-md` class to set a different maximum width.

